WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.399
Welcome to the first of
three lectures for the

2
00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:08.357
navigation-systems partial exam of
Transport Aeri i Sistemes de Navegació.

3
00:00:08.407 --> 00:00:12.535
This part covers air navigation: the
vocabulary, the charts, the wind

4
00:00:12.575 --> 00:00:17.697
triangle, and every navigation problem
type that has appeared in past partials.

5
00:00:17.747 --> 00:00:20.768
Parts two and three cover
the telecommunication

6
00:00:20.808 --> 00:00:23.764
fundamentals: signals
and systems, noise, data

7
00:00:23.804 --> 00:00:28.778
transmission, analogue to digital
conversion, propagation and antennas.

8
00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:32.625
Every worked answer in these videos
has been recomputed and checked

9
00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:37.493
against Professor Barlabé's answer
key where one exists; the 2022 and

10
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:42.866
2023 papers have no key, so those
answers were recomputed independently.

11
00:00:44.516 --> 00:00:47.344
Let us start with the rules
of the game, as announced

12
00:00:47.384 --> 00:00:50.764
by Professor Barlabé on the
twenty-second of October.

13
00:00:50.814 --> 00:00:54.657
The exam covers everything from
the first topic up to analogue to

14
00:00:54.697 --> 00:00:59.349
digital and digital to analogue
conversion, so passband signals are out.

15
00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:02.726
It lasts about an hour, and
you may bring only a normal

16
00:01:02.766 --> 00:01:06.032
scientific calculator: no
tables and no formula sheet,

17
00:01:06.072 --> 00:01:09.850
so every formula in these
videos must live in your head.

18
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.273
The past papers vary: 2016 had
one navigation problem and nine

19
00:01:14.313 --> 00:01:18.625
short questions, 2022 one
navigation problem, a radio-link

20
00:01:18.665 --> 00:01:23.099
problem and seven short questions,
and 2023 two navigation

21
00:01:23.139 --> 00:01:28.454
problems, a Friis link budget and nine
short questions, so be ready for any mix.

22
00:01:28.504 --> 00:01:32.479
This first partial is worth 20
% of the course, and anyone may

23
00:01:32.519 --> 00:01:36.772
retake it on the day of the final
exam to pass or to improve the mark.

24
00:01:38.422 --> 00:01:41.293
Navigation, in Barlabé's
words, is the art of

25
00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:45.018
getting from one place to
another without getting lost.

26
00:01:45.068 --> 00:01:49.212
Historically it began with observation
of landmarks and stars, and with

27
00:01:49.252 --> 00:01:53.963
dead reckoning, which is estimating
position from heading, speed and time.

28
00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:57.300
Then came radio: direction
finding, and radio beacons

29
00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.626
such as the non-directional
beacon with its automatic

30
00:02:00.666 --> 00:02:05.033
direction finder, the VOR, the
DME and the military TACAN.

31
00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:08.380
Air-traffic control added
radar: primary radar sees

32
00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:13.154
reflections, secondary radar
interrogates the aircraft transponder.

33
00:02:13.204 --> 00:02:18.706
Satellite systems such as GPS, GLONASS
and Galileo are broadcast systems,

34
00:02:18.746 --> 00:02:24.033
while Argos or Inmarsat C work point
to point; and there is a long list of

35
00:02:24.073 --> 00:02:30.109
aids such as ILS, collision avoidance,
radio altimeters, ADS-B and ACARS.

36
00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:34.901
Finally, augmentation systems such
as EGNOS or differential GPS improve

37
00:02:34.941 --> 00:02:40.262
accuracy, and inertial platforms navigate
without any telecommunication at all.

38
00:02:41.912 --> 00:02:46.036
Positions on the Earth are given by
latitude, the angle north or south of

39
00:02:46.076 --> 00:02:50.818
the equator, and longitude, the angle
east or west of the Greenwich meridian.

40
00:02:50.868 --> 00:02:55.342
One minute of arc of a great circle, for
example of a meridian, measures about

41
00:02:55.382 --> 00:03:02.561
one nautical mile, and the nautical mile
itself is defined as exactly 1 852 m.

42
00:03:02.611 --> 00:03:06.010
So one degree of latitude is 60 nmi, and a

43
00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:09.532
speed of one knot means one
nautical mile per hour.

44
00:03:09.582 --> 00:03:12.562
This gives a very fast
trick for problems where an

45
00:03:12.602 --> 00:03:15.340
aircraft flies along a
meridian: the change of

46
00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:19.534
latitude in minutes is directly
the distance in nautical miles.

47
00:03:19.584 --> 00:03:24.187
Be careful along a parallel, though:
meridians converge towards the poles, so

48
00:03:24.227 --> 00:03:29.582
one minute of longitude measures only the
cosine of the latitude in nautical miles.

49
00:03:31.232 --> 00:03:34.901
A chart represents a sphere on
a flat sheet of paper, so it

50
00:03:34.941 --> 00:03:39.001
needs a projection, and no
projection preserves everything.

51
00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:42.639
The loxodrome, or rhumb line,
is the curve that cuts every

52
00:03:42.679 --> 00:03:47.470
meridian at the same angle: flying
it means keeping a constant heading.

53
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.865
On a Mercator chart, a conformal
cylindrical projection, the

54
00:03:50.905 --> 00:03:54.696
rhumb line is a straight line,
which is why sailors loved it.

55
00:03:54.746 --> 00:03:59.039
The orthodrome, or great circle, is the
arc of the largest circle through the

56
00:03:59.079 --> 00:04:05.000
two points, shorter than 180 degrees, and
it is the shortest route between them.

57
00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:09.040
Look at Barcelona to New York on the
Mercator chart: the great circle bows

58
00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:13.515
north, towards the pole, while the rhumb
line stays straight but is longer.

59
00:04:13.565 --> 00:04:17.208
On the Lambert conformal conic
projection, the great circle is

60
00:04:17.248 --> 00:04:20.951
approximately a straight line,
which is why aeronautical charts

61
00:04:20.991 --> 00:04:25.084
use it, and polar regions use a
polar stereographic projection.

62
00:04:25.134 --> 00:04:28.486
A theory question that students
reported from the partial

63
00:04:28.526 --> 00:04:31.819
asks exactly this difference
between a loxodromic and an

64
00:04:31.859 --> 00:04:35.840
orthodromic line, so learn the
two definitions word for word.

65
00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:43.285
Now the vocabulary, which the exam uses
in Catalan, so I will give both names.

66
00:04:43.335 --> 00:04:46.942
The heading, rumb in Catalan, is
the direction in which the nose

67
00:04:46.982 --> 00:04:51.092
of the aircraft points, and it is
what the compass shows the pilot.

68
00:04:51.142 --> 00:04:54.475
The track, or derrota, is
the projection of the real

69
00:04:54.515 --> 00:04:58.621
trajectory on the ground: where
the aircraft actually goes.

70
00:04:58.671 --> 00:05:01.541
The drift, or deriva, is the angle between

71
00:05:01.581 --> 00:05:05.035
heading and track, and it
is caused by the wind.

72
00:05:05.085 --> 00:05:08.731
The bearing, demora, is the
direction in which you see an

73
00:05:08.771 --> 00:05:12.917
object, measured clockwise from
north, while the marcació, the

74
00:05:12.957 --> 00:05:17.869
relative bearing, is measured from the
longitudinal axis of the aircraft.

75
00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:22.131
All these angles go clockwise from
north, as on a compass rose, with

76
00:05:22.171 --> 00:05:26.321
three digits: east is zero nine
zero, south one eight zero and west

77
00:05:26.361 --> 00:05:30.698
two seven zero, and in aviation
they are magnetic, although Barlabé

78
00:05:30.738 --> 00:05:35.277
notes that in Catalonia the magnetic
declination is now negligible.

79
00:05:35.327 --> 00:05:39.120
The magnetic declination is the
angle between true and magnetic

80
00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:42.830
north, counted positive when
magnetic north lies east of true

81
00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:46.602
north and negative when it lies
west; runway numbers and VOR

82
00:05:46.642 --> 00:05:51.676
radials are magnetic, which is why
aeronautical charts print the declination.

83
00:05:53.326 --> 00:05:58.030
Every navigation problem in this course
reduces to one triangle of vectors.

84
00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.876
The air vector points along the heading,
with a length equal to the true air speed.

85
00:06:02.926 --> 00:06:06.567
The wind vector has the wind speed
as its length, but here is the

86
00:06:06.607 --> 00:06:10.134
classic trap: a wind is named by
where it comes FROM, so a wind

87
00:06:10.174 --> 00:06:17.545
from 360 blows towards 180, and
the vector must point towards 180.

88
00:06:17.595 --> 00:06:21.486
Adding the two gives the ground
vector, whose direction is the track

89
00:06:21.526 --> 00:06:26.269
and whose length is the ground speed,
which is exactly what the GPS shows.

90
00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:30.663
The safest way to add vectors with
a calculator is by components: with

91
00:06:30.703 --> 00:06:35.110
x pointing east and y pointing north,
a vector of size V at angle theta

92
00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:40.009
has an east component V sine theta
and a north component V cosine theta.

93
00:06:40.059 --> 00:06:43.601
To get an angle back, take the
arc tangent of east over north

94
00:06:43.641 --> 00:06:47.065
and correct the quadrant;
alternatively, solve the triangle

95
00:06:47.105 --> 00:06:50.647
with the sine and cosine laws,
which the problem sheet prints

96
00:06:50.687 --> 00:06:54.736
at the bottom of the page and
which you must now know by heart.

97
00:06:56.386 --> 00:06:59.585
Watch the triangle being built
for the first problem of the

98
00:06:59.625 --> 00:07:06.674
collection: heading 045 at 100 kts,
with a north wind, from 360, at 20 kts.

99
00:07:06.724 --> 00:07:12.194
First the air vector: 100 kts along
zero four five, to the north-east.

100
00:07:12.244 --> 00:07:15.429
At its tip we add the wind:
20 kts pointing south,

101
00:07:15.469 --> 00:07:19.850
towards 180, because the
wind comes from the north.

102
00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:22.455
The vector that closes
the triangle, from the

103
00:07:22.495 --> 00:07:25.498
origin to the final tip,
is the ground vector.

104
00:07:25.548 --> 00:07:28.417
The north wind pushes the
aircraft to the right of its

105
00:07:28.457 --> 00:07:31.433
heading, so the track is
larger than 045, and the

106
00:07:31.473 --> 00:07:35.755
headwind component makes the
ground speed smaller than 100 kts.

107
00:07:35.805 --> 00:07:39.147
The numbers come out as
a track of 054, a ground

108
00:07:39.187 --> 00:07:43.550
speed of 87 kts, and a drift
of 9 degrees to the right.

109
00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:49.428
Let us solve it by components,
the method that never fails.

110
00:07:49.478 --> 00:07:55.947
The air vector at 045 and 100 kts has
east and north components of 70.7 each.

111
00:07:55.997 --> 00:08:01.065
The wind comes from 360,
so it blows towards 180:

112
00:08:01.105 --> 00:08:04.474
its components are zero
east and minus 20 north.

113
00:08:04.524 --> 00:08:09.761
Adding them, the ground vector
is 70.7 east and 50.7 north.

114
00:08:09.811 --> 00:08:14.926
The track is the arc tangent
of 70.7 over 50.7, which is

115
00:08:14.966 --> 00:08:20.471
54.4 degrees, so track 054,
exactly the official answer.

116
00:08:20.521 --> 00:08:26.339
And as a bonus, the ground speed is the
square root of the sum of squares: 87 kts.

117
00:08:27.989 --> 00:08:30.353
This is the inverse
problem: we know the air

118
00:08:30.393 --> 00:08:33.529
vector and the ground vector,
and we want the wind.

119
00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:39.583
The air vector is heading 000 at
100 kts: zero east, 100 north.

120
00:08:39.633 --> 00:08:43.168
The GPS gives the ground
vector: 110 kts on

121
00:08:43.208 --> 00:08:49.120
track 010, which is 19.1
east and 108.3 north.

122
00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:51.626
Since ground equals air
plus wind, the wind

123
00:08:51.666 --> 00:08:56.254
is ground minus air:
19.1 east and 8.3 north.

124
00:08:56.304 --> 00:09:01.182
Its size is 20.8 kts and it
points towards 066 degrees.

125
00:09:01.232 --> 00:09:04.550
But meteorologists and pilots
name the wind by where it comes

126
00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:10.661
FROM, so we add 180: the wind
is from 246 at about 21 kts, a

127
00:09:10.701 --> 00:09:14.794
south-westerly that pushes us to
the right and slightly from behind.

128
00:09:16.444 --> 00:09:19.697
Here the track is imposed,
zero six zero, and we must

129
00:09:19.737 --> 00:09:23.876
find the heading that makes the
wind push us exactly onto it.

130
00:09:23.926 --> 00:09:28.436
The wind comes from 315, the
north-west, so it blows towards

131
00:09:28.476 --> 00:09:33.599
135 and pushes the aircraft to
the right of the desired track.

132
00:09:33.649 --> 00:09:37.565
Only the crosswind component matters
for the heading: 20 kts times the

133
00:09:37.605 --> 00:09:43.473
sine of the 75 degree angle between the
wind and the track, which is 19.3 kts.

134
00:09:43.523 --> 00:09:46.444
The air vector must cancel
this crosswind, so the wind

135
00:09:46.484 --> 00:09:52.812
correction angle is the arc sine of
19.3 over 100, which is 11.1 degrees.

136
00:09:52.862 --> 00:09:57.299
Because the wind pushes us right, we
turn the nose left, into the wind:

137
00:09:57.339 --> 00:10:05.948
heading 060 minus 11.1 gives 048.9, so
heading 049, which is the official answer.

138
00:10:05.998 --> 00:10:09.430
As a check, the along-track
components give a ground speed of

139
00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:14.487
about 103 kts, since this wind
has a small tailwind component.

140
00:10:16.137 --> 00:10:19.382
In the 2023 paper both
the track and the ground

141
00:10:19.422 --> 00:10:22.687
speed are imposed, so we solve
for the full air vector.

142
00:10:22.737 --> 00:10:26.702
The desired ground vector, 200 kts on 130,

143
00:10:26.742 --> 00:10:33.466
has components 153.2 east
and minus 128.6 north.

144
00:10:33.516 --> 00:10:36.547
The wind from 020 blows towards 200,

145
00:10:36.587 --> 00:10:41.146
giving minus 8.6 east
and minus 23.5 north.

146
00:10:41.196 --> 00:10:49.104
The air vector is ground minus wind:
161.8 east and minus 105.1 north.

147
00:10:49.154 --> 00:10:53.309
Its direction is 123 degrees,
south-east, so the pilot flies

148
00:10:53.349 --> 00:10:57.563
heading 123; notice the arc
tangent alone would give an angle

149
00:10:57.603 --> 00:11:01.729
in the wrong quadrant, which is
why you must look at the signs.

150
00:11:01.779 --> 00:11:07.242
Its size is the true air speed to
set: about 193 kts, a little less

151
00:11:07.282 --> 00:11:13.193
than 200 because this wind has a
small tailwind component along 130.

152
00:11:14.843 --> 00:11:18.549
This one rewards knowing the
definition of the nautical mile.

153
00:11:18.599 --> 00:11:22.479
The longitude does not change and
the latitude increases, so the

154
00:11:22.519 --> 00:11:29.363
aircraft flies due north along the
meridian: heading 000, also written 360.

155
00:11:29.413 --> 00:11:35.127
The change of latitude is 1 degree 25
minutes, which is 85 minutes of arc.

156
00:11:35.177 --> 00:11:38.533
Since one minute of latitude
is one nautical mile, the

157
00:11:38.573 --> 00:11:43.514
distance is exactly 85 nmi,
without any trigonometry.

158
00:11:43.564 --> 00:11:46.147
Fifteen minutes is a
quarter of an hour, so the

159
00:11:46.187 --> 00:11:51.670
speed is 85 divided by
0.25, which is 340 kts.

160
00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:56.994
A radial is the direction in which
the wave travels away from the

161
00:11:57.034 --> 00:12:01.042
beacon, so the aircraft lies on
that line, seen from the beacon.

162
00:12:01.092 --> 00:12:04.542
With the beacon at the origin,
the first position is 10

163
00:12:04.582 --> 00:12:11.786
nmi along 190: about minus 1.74
east and minus 9.85 north.

164
00:12:11.836 --> 00:12:21.033
The second position is 14 nmi along 210:
minus 7.00 east and minus 12.12 north.

165
00:12:21.083 --> 00:12:27.320
The displacement in that minute is
minus 5.26 east and minus 2.28 north.

166
00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:31.447
Its length is 5.73 nmi,
flown in one minute,

167
00:12:31.487 --> 00:12:37.251
so the speed is 5.73 times
60, which is 344 kts.

168
00:12:37.301 --> 00:12:41.366
Its direction, in the south-west
quadrant because both components are

169
00:12:41.406 --> 00:12:48.240
negative, is 246.6 degrees: track
247, matching the official answer.

170
00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:52.235
Here there is no wind: it is pure vector

171
00:12:52.275 --> 00:12:55.905
addition, which is exactly
what a drone flight plan is.

172
00:12:55.955 --> 00:13:03.620
The first leg, 100 m on 310, is
minus 76.6 east and 64.3 north.

173
00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:11.833
The second leg, 300 m on 030,
is 150.0 east and 259.8 north.

174
00:13:11.883 --> 00:13:18.967
So waypoint 2 lies 73.4 m east
and 324.1 m north of the start.

175
00:13:19.017 --> 00:13:24.092
The distance home is the
length of that vector, 332 m.

176
00:13:24.142 --> 00:13:27.417
The way home is the opposite
vector, pointing south and

177
00:13:27.457 --> 00:13:31.816
slightly west, which gives heading
193, the official answer; a

178
00:13:31.856 --> 00:13:35.613
quick sanity check: waypoint 2
lies on bearing zero one three

179
00:13:35.653 --> 00:13:40.282
from the start, and the reciprocal
of zero one three is one nine three.

180
00:13:41.932 --> 00:13:45.859
The interception problem appeared
as problem 1.2 of the collection

181
00:13:45.899 --> 00:13:50.269
and as question 1 of the November
2016 partial, worth two points.

182
00:13:50.319 --> 00:13:56.381
At time zero a target at 42 degrees
north flies heading 225 at 500 kts.

183
00:13:56.431 --> 00:14:00.843
An interceptor at the same
latitude, 300 nmi to the west,

184
00:14:00.883 --> 00:14:05.570
flies at 1 000 kts and must choose
a heading that meets the target.

185
00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:09.670
Both fly straight lines at constant
speed, so after the unknown time t

186
00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:15.084
the target has flown 500 t nautical
miles and the interceptor 1 000 t.

187
00:14:15.134 --> 00:14:19.703
These two distances and the
initial 300 nmi separation form a

188
00:14:19.743 --> 00:14:23.131
triangle, and the angle at the
target between the line to the

189
00:14:23.171 --> 00:14:30.658
interceptor, due west at 270, and the
target's heading, 225, is 45 degrees.

190
00:14:30.708 --> 00:14:35.493
Watch the two aircraft converge on the
meeting point after about 14 minutes.

191
00:14:37.143 --> 00:14:40.815
Barlabé's own hint in the FAQ
is: it is just a triangle,

192
00:14:40.855 --> 00:14:44.854
use the cosine law for t and
the sine law for the heading.

193
00:14:44.904 --> 00:14:48.741
The side opposite the 45 degree
angle is the interceptor's

194
00:14:48.781 --> 00:14:53.030
path, so the cosine law gives
1 000 t squared equals 300

195
00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:59.941
squared plus 500 t squared minus two
times 300 times 500 t times cosine 45.

196
00:14:59.991 --> 00:15:03.809
Rearranging, we get a quadratic: 750 000 t

197
00:15:03.849 --> 00:15:10.418
squared plus 212 132 t
minus 90 000 equals zero.

198
00:15:10.468 --> 00:15:13.906
Its positive root is t equal to 0.233

199
00:15:13.946 --> 00:15:17.355
hours, about 14 minutes,
the official answer.

200
00:15:17.405 --> 00:15:20.925
Now the sine law: the sine of
the angle at the interceptor,

201
00:15:20.965 --> 00:15:25.571
between due east and its heading,
equals 500 t times sine 45

202
00:15:25.611 --> 00:15:32.418
divided by 1 000 t, which is 0.354,
so the angle is 20.7 degrees.

203
00:15:32.468 --> 00:15:35.231
The target is south-east
of the interceptor, so the

204
00:15:35.271 --> 00:15:39.352
interceptor turns 20.7 degrees
to the right of 090, giving

205
00:15:39.392 --> 00:15:45.183
110.7 degrees, which rounds to
heading 111, as in the answer key.

206
00:15:46.833 --> 00:15:50.503
Let me close part one with
the traps that cost points.

207
00:15:50.553 --> 00:15:53.886
First, the wind: it is always
named by where it comes

208
00:15:53.926 --> 00:15:59.367
from, so turn it around by 180
degrees before adding vectors.

209
00:15:59.417 --> 00:16:02.873
Second, always draw a sketch
before calculating: it tells you

210
00:16:02.913 --> 00:16:07.104
the quadrant, and whether the drift
is to the left or to the right.

211
00:16:07.154 --> 00:16:10.515
Third, work in components and
decide the quadrant of the

212
00:16:10.555 --> 00:16:14.610
final angle from the signs, never
from the arc tangent alone.

213
00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:17.953
Fourth, to correct a drift you
point the nose into the wind,

214
00:16:17.993 --> 00:16:22.010
and a headwind lowers the ground
speed while a tailwind raises it.

215
00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:25.215
Fifth, a radial is the direction
in which the wave leaves

216
00:16:25.255 --> 00:16:28.912
the beacon, and a bearing is
measured clockwise from north.

217
00:16:28.962 --> 00:16:32.375
Sixth, keep nautical miles
and knots, remember that a

218
00:16:32.415 --> 00:16:37.132
degree of latitude is 60 nmi,
and convert minutes to hours;

219
00:16:37.172 --> 00:16:41.758
and finally, give directions with
three digits, as aviation does.

220
00:16:43.408 --> 00:16:47.595
To summarise part one: you now know
the navigation families, the two kinds

221
00:16:47.635 --> 00:16:52.384
of route on a chart, and the vocabulary
of heading, track, drift and bearing.

222
00:16:52.434 --> 00:16:56.297
Every problem is the same
triangle, ground equals air plus

223
00:16:56.337 --> 00:17:00.725
wind, solved by components or
with the sine and cosine laws.

224
00:17:00.775 --> 00:17:04.689
We have solved every navigation problem
from the collection and from the

225
00:17:04.729 --> 00:17:10.674
2016, 2022 and 2023 partials, and
every answer agrees with the official

226
00:17:10.714 --> 00:17:15.127
key where one exists, or was recomputed
independently where there is none.

227
00:17:15.177 --> 00:17:19.891
Before the exam, redo problems
1.1 to 1.5 on paper without

228
00:17:19.931 --> 00:17:24.072
looking, and time yourself: each
should take under ten minutes.

229
00:17:24.122 --> 00:17:27.420
In part two we move to
telecommunications: signals,

230
00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:32.065
Fourier, linear systems, distortion,
noise and the Shannon limit.
